Electrolytes allow an electric current to pass, and yet many of
 them are transparent. We may suppose, however, that in the
 case of the rapidly alternating forces which come into play
 during the propagation of light, the electromotive intensity acts
 for so short a time in one direction that it is unable to effect
 a complete separation between the combined molecules. When,
 during the other half of the vibration, the electromotive intensity
 acts in the opposite direction it simply reverses what it did
 during the first half. There is thus no true conduction through
 the electrolyte, no loss of electric energy, and consequently no
 absorption of light.

