827.] The components, $X$ and $Y$, of the impressed force, re
ferred to unit of volume, may be deduced from this by Lagrange's
 equations, Art. 564. We observe that by two successive inte
grations by parts in regard to $z$, and the omission of the double
 integrals at the bounding surface, it may be shewn that

$$\iiint \frac{d^{2} \xi}{d z^{2}} \dot{\eta} d x d y d z=\iiint \xi \frac{d^{3} \eta}{d z^{2} d t} d x d y d z$$


Hence

$$\frac{d T}{d \xi}=C_{\gamma} \frac{d^{3} \eta}{d z^{2} d t} .$$


The expressions for the forces are therefore given by

$$\begin{align*}
& X=\rho \frac{d^{2} \xi}{d t^{2}}-2 C \gamma \frac{d^{3} \eta}{d z^{2} d t}  \tag{10}\\
& Y=\rho \frac{d^{2} \eta}{d t^{2}}+2 C \gamma \frac{d^{3} \xi}{d z^{2} d t} \tag{11}
\end{align*}$$


These forces arise from the action of the remainder of the
 medium on the element under consideration, and must in the case
 of an isotropic medium be of the form indicated by Cauchy,

$$\begin{align*}
& X=A_{0} \frac{d^{2} \xi}{d z^{2}}+A_{1} \frac{d^{4} \xi}{d z^{4}}+\text { \&c. }  \tag{12}\\
& Y=A_{0} \frac{d^{2} \eta}{d z^{2}}+A_{1} \frac{d^{4} \eta}{d z^{4}}+\text { \&c. } \tag{13}
\end{align*}$$

828.] If we now take the case of a circularly-polarized ray for
 which

$$\begin{equation*}
\xi=r \cos (n t-q z), \quad \eta=r \sin (n t-q z), \tag{14}
\end{equation*}$$

we find for the kinetic energy in unit of volume

$$\begin{equation*}
T=\frac{1}{2} \rho r^{2} n^{2}-C \gamma r^{2} q^{2} n ; \tag{15}
\end{equation*}$$

and for the potential energy in unit of volume

$$\begin{align*}
V & =\frac{1}{2} r^{2}\left(A_{0} q^{2}-A_{1} q^{4}+\& \mathrm{c} .\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2} r^{2} Q, \tag{16}
\end{align*}$$

where $Q$ is a function of $q^{2}$.
